---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-with-magnesium`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D With Magnesium',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D With Magnesium', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D With Magnesium: Why the Cofactor Matters and When to Combine"
  description="Magnesium is a required cofactor for vitamin D activation. Deficiency of one worsens the other — when combining supplementation makes sense and the best magnesium forms."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-with-magnesium", label: "Vitamin D With Magnesium" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D With Magnesium</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Every enzyme in the vitamin D activation cascade — 25-hydroxylase, 1α-hydroxylase,
      24-hydroxylase, and vitamin D receptor binding — requires magnesium. Chronic
      magnesium deficiency limits how effectively the body can activate vitamin D, and
      is one reason some patients don't respond to supplementation as expected.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The activation cascade</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cholecalciferol (D3) → 25(OH)D in the liver (25-hydroxylase, Mg-dependent) →
      1,25(OH)₂D in the kidney (1α-hydroxylase, Mg-dependent). Vitamin D receptor
      transactivation and vitamin D binding protein synthesis also require magnesium.
      Severely magnesium-deficient patients can have low 25(OH)D that fails to rise on
      standard cholecalciferol doses until magnesium is corrected.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Prevalence of magnesium deficiency</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Half of US adults consume less than the RDA (310–420 mg/day). NHANES data
      suggests ~48% of Americans get less than the estimated average requirement.
      Chronic subclinical deficiency is common but rarely tested — serum magnesium is a
      poor marker (only 1% of body magnesium is extracellular).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Combined dosing</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Vitamin D 2,000 IU/day + magnesium 300–400 mg/day</strong> is a reasonable general adult stack.</li>
      <li><strong>Preferred magnesium forms:</strong> glycinate (well tolerated, no GI effects), citrate (mild osmotic laxative — helpful if constipated), malate.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid magnesium oxide</strong> for repletion — poor bioavailability (~4%).</li>
      <li><strong>Take separately from calcium</strong> — they compete for absorption.</li>
      <li><strong>Take with food</strong> — vitamin D absorbs better with fat.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Signs that suggest combined deficiency</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Muscle cramps, especially in calves overnight</li>
      <li>Twitching, restless legs</li>
      <li>Palpitations without heart disease</li>
      <li>Constipation</li>
      <li>Difficulty raising 25(OH)D despite adequate cholecalciferol dosing</li>
      <li>Persistent secondary hyperparathyroidism despite normal calcium</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vitamin K2 — the third partner</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Some stacks add K2 (MK-7). The rationale: vitamin D increases calcium absorption;
      K2 activates osteocalcin (directing calcium to bone) and matrix Gla protein
      (inhibiting vascular calcification). Evidence for combined D+K2 improving cardiovascular
      or bone outcomes over D+Mg alone is limited — see our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-magnesium-k2" class="text-amber-700 underline">D + Mg + K2 page</a>.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Chronic kidney disease patients: don't add
      magnesium supplements without checking with your nephrologist — CKD alters
      magnesium homeostasis.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-with-magnesium" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
